WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.599
In the spirit of the Olympic Games going on
right now, I went and found an entrance exam

00:00:04.599 --> 00:00:09.200
from the University of Tokyo. I thought it
would be interesting to see how things are

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.519
done in Japan. But to be honest, it turned
out to be a frightening and stressful experience.

00:00:14.519 --> 00:00:19.910
For some reason these questions I'm about
to show you scared me more than any other

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:25.090
exam I've looked at recently, and they're
only at the undergraduate level to gain entrance

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:30.800
to the university. In Japan, students applying
to public universities will first take the

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.850
National Center test. And with these scores,
they can then apply to particularly universities,

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:41.739
where they might be invited to take a department
specific test. The University of Tokyo is

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:48.560
one of Japan's top ranking universities and
this is their 2021 mathematics test. It's

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.780
notorious enough that it's inspired a couple
of animes, and researchers are even working

00:00:53.780 --> 00:01:00.089
on a robot that can pass this exam. Professor
Noriko Arai said that the robot is already

00:01:00.089 --> 00:01:07.410
scoring in the top 1%. But perhaps what this
means is that our exams often focus too much

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:12.870
on things that can be memorised - even by
a computer, and instead should be focusing

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:19.720
on understanding, which is where we still
have an advantage over the AI. For this exam,

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:27.020
students have 150 minutes. And with six questions,
that's 25 minutes per question. I've translated

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:33.030
them into English so let's have a read through
them. For question one, a and b are real numbers

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:39.380
and we have this parabola C, which has two
shared points with another parabola, y is

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:45.530
equal to minus x squared. The x coordinate
of one shared point is between minus one and

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:52.410
zero. And the other shared points x coordinate
is between zero and one. Part one asks to

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:58.770
draw the possible ranges of the points a and
b on the coordinate plane. And Part two is

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:03.950
to draw the possible paths of the parabola
C on the coordinate plane. And the solutions

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:12.080
for this question look a bit like this for
part one, and part two. In question two, it's

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:18.769
all about complex numbers, we have this equation
f of z is equal to a z squared plus b z plus

00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:27.330
c. a, b and c are complex numbers, and i is
the imaginary unit. For part one, let alpha,

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:34.599
beta and gamma be complex numbers. If f of
zero is alpha, f of one is beta, and f of

00:02:34.599 --> 00:02:42.790
i is gamma, then denote a, b and c by alpha,
beta and gamma respectively. Part Two asks

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:49.700
if f of zero, f of one and f of i are all
real numbers between one and two, graphically

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:55.660
represent the possible range of f of two on
the complex number plane. Now I'm going to

00:02:55.660 --> 00:03:00.659
come back in a moment and show my full solution
to this one. But in the meantime, maybe have

00:03:00.659 --> 00:03:05.790
a think about it. I have only used online
tools for the translation. So that might make

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:10.700
it a little harder than it needs to be to
understand but these still seem really difficult

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:16.170
to me, at least, it seems hard to form a picture
in my mind of how this solution is going to

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:22.900
play out. Here's question three, we have a
function called C, a tangent to that function

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:28.660
at a point A. And we're asked to show that
there's only one other shared point between

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:33.890
C and the tangent that is different from A,
and to find the coordinate of that point.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:40.780
Then for part two, we're using that x coordinate
and having to calculate an integral. An online

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:46.959
solution I found for this one shows pages
of integrals. And I just thought, excuse me,

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:54.450
what on earth? With 25 minutes per question,
they really can ask some quite involved calculations.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:59.430
This fourth question was an absolute mind
bender. It uses this notation here, which

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:04.260
I believe refers to a binomial coefficient.
And I think I would need a lot more practice

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:09.680
with those to really understand how to do
this question. Question five here is kind

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:15.010
of about examining the increase and decrease
of a function, and in doing that, finding

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:21.100
derivatives. And then there's question six
here, which I found the hardest to understand.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:25.610
Maybe that's the translation or maybe I'm
just not used to doing problems that are set

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:31.090
out like this. Either way, I think I can be
confident that it's pretty difficult. But

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:36.360
let's jump back to question two, and I'll
show you my solution to that one. So here's

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:41.450
question two, we have this equation f of z.
We are told that alpha, beta and gamma are

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:48.530
these particular three values of f of z. And
we're asked to denote a, b and c using alpha,

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:54.620
beta and gamma. So we can take what they've
told us about f zero, and set z to zero in

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:59.320
the equation. And that would give us c is
equal to alpha, which is the first of the

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:05.660
three done and definitely the easiest one.
Next, we can set z to be one, and that would

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:12.090
be equal to beta. But we don't get any further
than knowing that a plus b is equal to beta

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:17.290
minus alpha. So we take a look at the third
piece of information we're given and set z

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:24.640
to i. That will give us this equation and
rearranging it, we get minus a plus bi is

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:30.790
gamma minus alpha. So we've ended up with
two simultaneous equations to solve for a

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:37.810
and b. We can solve them by elimination to
find out what our value of b is in terms of

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:43.800
alpha, beta and gamma. Now that we know b,
we can sub it back and find a, which has a

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:48.640
similar looking form. I will say the process
up to here involves quite a bit of algebra

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:54.300
and manipulation of i and negative numbers
all over the place, which leads plenty of

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:58.580
room to make a mistake, what you're not seeing
on this page is all the times I screwed it

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:03.980
up myself. But this is the answer for part
one, we've represented a, b and c as they

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:09.870
wanted us to. Then for part two, they're saying
that alpha, beta and gamma are all real numbers

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:15.560
between one and two. And if we set z to be
equal to two, they want us to graphically

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:22.220
represent the range of that function. So if
we set z to two, we get four a plus two b

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:29.980
plus c, then if we sub in our previous answers
for a, b, and c, we get this pretty long equation

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:36.450
here. But we can simplify it all the way down
to this line. And what's useful to notice

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:42.380
is that whilst alpha, beta and gamma are just
real numbers between one and two, these coefficients

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:49.290
in front of them are three complex numbers.
So the function is the addition of three complex

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:55.720
numbers. And we can even call them z one,
z two, and z three, we want to plot the range

00:06:55.720 --> 00:07:01.050
of this function. And we can actually just
find the vertices directly by setting alpha,

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:07.700
beta and gamma to either one or two. Here's
how I've done it here. All the possible values

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:13.740
of alpha, beta and gamma, would give us a
combination that falls between these points.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:18.720
And I've plotted them here on the complex
plane, that gives us this kind of hexagon

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:25.880
shape. And the range of f two, which is what
they asked us for is the boundary and interior

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:32.540
of the shape. You can notice that when alpha,
beta and gamma are all equal to one, or all

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:38.180
equal to two, that point falls within the
shape, and so isn't actually necessary to

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:44.180
be able to draw the boundary. But you might
ask, why does the shape look like this? Well,

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:50.480
we can go back and consider the function with
just alpha and beta the first two terms, when

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:55.850
we add these terms together with alpha and
beta between one and two, we get something

00:07:55.850 --> 00:08:02.020
like this, it's actually a parallelogram of
possible solutions. Here is the point where

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:08.170
alpha and beta are one, and here is the point
where they're both two, then adding on that

00:08:08.170 --> 00:08:15.260
third term, z three gamma, that will transform
each point of the parallelogram in the direction

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:21.970
of z three, which is minus one plus i. And
that would be a transformation out in this

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:27.670
angle, essentially, to the left one and up
one. So here's what the parallelogram looks

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:33.620
like when it's transformed like that. If this
is where it was originally marked by P, then

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:39.360
it will first move up to here, when gamma
is equal to one, and its other limit will

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:46.080
be out here, when gamma is equal to two, and
so will exist in all the possible places between

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:53.529
these two points. It is just the same hexagon
shape that we had before. This kind of smeared

00:08:53.529 --> 00:08:59.980
out parallelogram shows the range of our solution.
I would say this exam deserves a gold medal

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:04.990
for difficulty. Of course, with a lot of practice,
it might start to look easier if you notice

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:10.430
similarities between a lot of past questions.
But getting into the University of Tokyo certainly

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:15.770
seems very competitive, and you at least need
to be very on top of your mathematics. Thanks

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:20.480
for watching this video. And thank you to
my Patreon supporters for making these videos

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:25.750
possible. And a special shout out to today's
Patron cat of the day Doorknob2.

